/T (1. CODE OPTIMIZATION TECHNIQUES)
/E (1. CODE OPTIMIZATION TECHNIQUES)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
419 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (A. VARIABLE USAGE: MINIMIZE THE USE OF GLOBAL VARIABLES AND USE LOCAL VARIABLES WHENEVER POSSIBLE TO CONSERVE MEMORY.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
420 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (B. DATA TYPES: CHOOSE THE APPROPRIATE DATA TYPES TO MINIMIZE MEMORY USAGE. FOR EXAMPLE, USE UINT8_T INSTEAD OF INT FOR VARIABLES THAT STORE VALUES BETWEEN 0 AND 255.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
421 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (C. AVOID STRING MANIPULATION: AVOID USING THE STRING CLASS FOR MANIPULATING STRINGS, AS IT CAN LEAD TO MEMORY FRAGMENTATION. INSTEAD, USE CHARACTER ARRAYS \(C-STRINGS\) FOR BETTER MEMORY MANAGEMENT.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
422 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (D. OPTIMIZED LOOPS: USE EFFICIENT LOOP STRUCTURES AND ALGORITHMS TO MINIMIZE EXECUTION TIME. FOR EXAMPLE, PREFER FOR LOOPS OVER WHILE LOOPS AND USE LOOP UNROLLING FOR REPETITIVE TASKS.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
423 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (E. LIBRARY SELECTION: CHOOSE LIGHTWEIGHT LIBRARIES THAT MEET YOUR PROJECT'S REQUIREMENTS TO MINIMIZE CODE SIZE AND MEMORY USAGE.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
424 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (2. MEMORY MANAGEMENT)
/E (2. MEMORY MANAGEMENT)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
425 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (A. STATIC MEMORY ALLOCATION: ALLOCATE MEMORY STATICALLY WHENEVER POSSIBLE TO AVOID DYNAMIC MEMORY ALLOCATION \(E.G., USING THE NEW KEYWORD\), WHICH CAN LEAD TO MEMORY FRAGMENTATION.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
426 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (B. AVOID MEMORY LEAKS: ENSURE THAT MEMORY ALLOCATED DYNAMICALLY IS PROPERLY DEALLOCATED USING THE DELETE KEYWORD TO PREVENT MEMORY LEAKS.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
427 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (C. MEMORY PROFILING: USE TOOLS SUCH AS THE MEMORYFREE LIBRARY OR THE MEMORY USAGE ANALYZER IN THE ARDUINO IDE TO MONITOR MEMORY USAGE AND IDENTIFY POTENTIAL MEMORY LEAKS OR INEFFICIENCIES.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
